index 00000000..17879703
--- /dev/null
+++ b/LOCAL_BROWSER.md
@@ -0,0 +1,76 @@
+# Local server browser edition
+
+This checkout adds a nonblocking browser to the shared SDL platform layer used by Android, Linux and Windows. The gameplay and invite-handshake protocols are unchanged (base commit a6ca914b8a7a98237787a8589e3da0b03f44a9dd, gameplay network version 7).
+
+## Android
+
+The local APK is **Halo CE Browser**, package `com.halo.decomp.browser`. It installs alongside the original app, with separate settings, game data and saves. Import your own Halo Xbox disc image again on first launch; the APK contains no game maps. When opening a `halo://` link, select Halo CE Browser if Android offers an app chooser. Self-updates are disabled for this unnumbered local build.
+
+Tap **Servers** at the top right. On desktop, use that button in the menus or press **F8**; Escape/Android Back closes the browser. Touch, mouse and keyboard opening/closing are supported. A controller continues to operate the game after the browser closes; controller navigation inside this overlay is not implemented.
+
+- **Private / Public: on** controls whether this device advertises its hosted System Link game. Publishing requires an explicit in-app choice. It resets to private on restart. Create a System Link game to host; opening the app alone does not host a game.
+- **Copy invite** copies your hosted game's link explicitly. Hosting no longer overwrites the clipboard.
+- **Add invite** reads a shared link from the clipboard and saves it on this device. The Add invite action does not publish it or contact its host. The pre-existing `network.join_from_clipboard` behavior can still connect when the app comes to the front; set it to `false` in config.toml if you want to connect only with Go/Join. Up to 32 saved links are supported. **Go** connects; **X** removes a saved link.
+- **Join** connects the existing P2P tunnel. Then close this panel and open **Multiplayer > System Link** to enter the game's normal lobby. The host still decides whether a player is accepted.
+- Public listings show lobby name, map, mode, host-reported players/capacity (including split screen), and open/full state. Previous/Next pages through five rows at a time.
+
+## Existing hosts and other builds
+
+Unchanged builds cannot be discovered globally: their signalling topics are derived from a secret invite token. You can save a link from an unchanged host and connect using it. Its row initially shows `?/?`; after connecting and searching System Link, valid game announcements supply its name, map, mode and count. These observed counts expire if announcements stop or the peer disconnects. A saved invite remains saved, even when offline; invites are only valid for the original host process's lifetime.
+
+Automatic discovery requires the host to run a build with this advertisement feature and explicitly make its lobby public. Joining players can still use an unchanged build with compatible networking by opening the normal invite link. Public advertisements filter mismatched gameplay protocol versions and player/machine limits. Saved invites may connect to a peer whose gameplay protocol is incompatible; the normal game compatibility check still applies, and an observed incompatible lobby is marked accordingly.
+
+## Discovery and privacy
+
+The existing configurable `network.signalling_brokers` are reused; no new directory or game server is deployed. Browsing subscribes to `hceu/browser-v1/#` only while this panel is open. Public hosts publish non-retained bounded binary advertisements every 10 seconds, and immediately when details change. Listings are deduplicated by host key hash across brokers and expire 35 seconds after the last live message. Private/stop-hosting transitions send a best-effort removal notice. A new browser may wait about 10 seconds for hosts to appear; a broker reconnect can add delay. The public cache holds at most 64 live hosts.
+
+Public metadata and invite tokens are visible to broker operators and subscribers. Listings and player counts are untrusted host reports, not verified directory claims. The existing invite handshake authenticates the peer key; publishing gives anyone the ability to request a join. Private games retain their existing invite-only signalling. Switching back to private removes the public listing but does not revoke links already obtained; restarting the host generates a new invite. Saved links are written only to `browser_invites.txt` in this app's save root; never advertised. Android's separate app sandbox protects that file; desktop users should protect their save folder as they would other invite secrets.
+
+The existing UDP hole-punching, STUN and UPnP behavior is unchanged. No relay was added, so restrictive NAT/mobile networks can still fail to connect. No public service, GitHub branch, pull request or release was created.
+
+## Build
+
+Use the original platform build prerequisites documented in the project READMEs:
+
+```sh
+python configure.py --release --portable --lto=off --pgo=off
+ninja linux          # Linux, with 32-bit SDL3
+ninja windows        # on Windows
+ninja android        # Android native libraries and guest image
+```
+
+For the separate local Android app, generate a local signing key once and package:
+
+```sh
+keytool -genkeypair -keystore build/android/browser-debug.keystore \
+  -storepass android -keypass android -alias androiddebugkey \
+  -dname 'CN=Android Debug,O=Android,C=US' -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000
+cd port/android
+./gradlew -PlocalBrowser assembleDebug
+```
+
+The resulting APK is `port/android/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk`. Keep the local key if you want to install subsequent local updates over this edition. Do not publish a private signing key. Omitting `-PlocalBrowser` preserves the original application identity. Standard builds have build number zero and do not self-update.
+
+## Validation
+
+- Android guest image and native libraries compile and link. The side-by-side APK assembles successfully, passes APK signature verification, and packages uncompressed native libraries at 16 KiB ZIP alignment. Its packaged guest image matches the final build.
+- Linux binary compiles and links with a locally built 32-bit SDL3 library; its gameplay has not been run here.
+- Browser protocol tests: boundary lengths/counts, invalid hexadecimal, string termination, compatibility, monotonic-clock wraparound, 10,000 malformed payloads under AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
+- Discovery tests with mocked transport: broker deduplication, crash expiry, stop/private removal, bounded flood handling, explicit publication, non-retained MQTT packets and changed counts under sanitizers.
+- UI harness: actual SDL/OpenGL offscreen rendering plus touch opening/closing, public opt-in, full-lobby protection, saved joins/removal and clipboard import. It uses sample fixtures, not real public games.
+
+No game assets or phone were available for an end-to-end gameplay test. Windows uses the same implementation but has not been built or run here. The overlay uses a compact ASCII font; non-ASCII lobby names are displayed as question marks.
+
+Run standalone protocol and UI checks from the source root:
+
+```sh
+clang -Iport/linux/src -Iport/linux/include -fsanitize=address,undefined \
+  tools/test_browser_protocol.c -o build/browser-protocol-test
+build/browser-protocol-test
+clang -O1 -DHALO_LINUX_PLATFORM_LAYER -Iport/linux/src -Iport/linux/include \
+  -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections -fsanitize=address,undefined \
+  tools/test_browser_discovery.c -o build/browser-discovery-test
+build/browser-discovery-test
+clang -Iport/linux/src tools/test_browser_ui.c -lSDL3 -lGL -o build/browser-ui-test
+SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen build/browser-ui-test build/browser-preview.bmp
+```
